Q: What was revenue in 2024 Q3, and what caused the Midwest decline?

=== what a shallow trace shows ===

  span                                         time tok in/out     cost
  clarity.request        ##################  5286ms                  
    model.call           ###                 1128ms  414/71  $0.00011
    tool.query_warehouse    ####             1388ms                  
    tool.search_documents        #####       1486ms                  
    model.call                        ####   1282ms 1065/93  $0.00022

  5 spans; 2410ms of 5286ms sits inside a span named model.* (46%)

=== the same trace, nothing re-run, nothing dropped ===

  clarity.request        ##################  5286ms                  
    model.call           ###                 1128ms  414/71  $0.00011
    tool.query_warehouse    ####             1388ms                  
      model.parse           ####             1316ms  739/65  $0.00015
    tool.search_documents        #####       1486ms                  
      model.embed                #            567ms   12/0           
      model.parse                  ###        914ms  173/22  $0.00004
    model.call                        ####   1282ms 1065/93  $0.00022

  8 spans, 5286ms end to end, $0.00051
  5206ms of it is a model call (98%)

  (dollar figures use placeholder rates — this book does not print a price
   list, because one printed with confidence is worse than none. Set RATE_*
   in .env from the provider's page and the numbers become yours.)

Read the shape first. The tool spans are siblings of the model calls, not
children, because the agent alternates: ask, run, ask again. A waterfall makes
that obvious in a way no log file does, and it is the first thing you want when
a run takes several seconds and nobody can say which part.

Then read the two shares. On the shallow view 46% of the run is model
latency and the rest is 'the tools being slow'. On the deep view it is 98%,
because 3 of those spans are model calls that were happening inside the tools
all along — the QuerySpec planner from §15.6, and an embedding call for the
query.

Nothing was re-run between those two blocks. It is one trace, filtered.

That is the sharpest thing tracing teaches, and it is not a technique. A trace
is only as deep as your instrumentation, and an uninstrumented call does not
show up as missing — it shows up as its caller being slow. Your first trace
tells you where your instrumentation stops. The second one starts telling you
about the system.
